\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 {\*\bkmkstart _tc3}{\*\bkmkend _tc3}{\v {\field{\*\fldinst TC "Installation 1:  Modifying WordStar" \\l 1}{\fldrslt }}}\b INSTALLATION 1:  MODIFYING WORDSTAR\b0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 \tab To use MICKEE, you must install WordStar to use the Help- Level-4 pulldown menus.  If you want WordStar's pulldowns to behave like proper mouse menus, you should use the new feature in WordStar 6.0 Rev. D that prevents cursor run-on in these menus.  If you want the cursor to behave like a proper mouse cursor in text mode\~-- traversing blank lines without snapping back to column 1\~-- you'll also want to turn on the new feature in WordStar 6.0 Rev. D that allows this.  Finally, to make Help- Level 4 more usable, you may want to turn on the option that allows classic commands to be issued directly from the Level 4 Opening Menu.  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 \tab To make these changes, run WINSTALL, the WordStar customization utility.  Select "Customize WordStar (WSCHANGE)."  Specify WS.EXE as the filename to modify and WS.EXE again as the filename to which changes should be saved.  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 \tab If you are using a Help Level other than 4 (that is, if you have installed WordStar for Classic menus), begin with step 1.  If you are already using Help-Level 4, begin with step 2.  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li720\ri0\fi-720\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 1.\tab Mandatory:  to activate use of WordStar's pulldown menus, go to WSCHANGE menu \b D\b0  (\i WordStar\i0 ), \b B\b0  (\i Editing settings\i0 ), \b A\b0  (\i Edit screen, help level\i0 ), \b A\b0  (\i Help level\i0 , which is label INIHLP).  Select \b 4\b0 .  Issue ^X to jump to the WSCHANGE exit point.  Answer "n" to the "Are you through making changes?" question.  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li720\ri0\fi-720\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li720\ri0\fi-720\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 2.\tab Highly recommended:  to prevent cursor run-on in the pulldown menus, go to WSCHANGE menu \b D\b0  (\i WordStar\i0 ), \b B\b0  (\i Editing settings\i0 ), \b A\b0  (\i Edit screen, help level\i0 ), \b 3\b0  (\i Edit screen, help level menu\~#3\i0 ), \b K\b0  (\i Turn cursor wrap on pull-downs on or off\i0 , which is label WRPPUL).  Select \b OFF\b0 .  Issue ^X to jump to the WSCHANGE exit point.  Answer "n" to the "Are you through making changes?" question.  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li720\ri0\fi-720\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li720\ri0\fi-720\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160\keep{\f0\fs24 3.\tab Recommended:  To make the cursor not snap back to column 1 when passing over a blank line, go to WSCHANGE menu \b D\b0  (\i WordStar\i0 ), \b B\b0  (\i Editing settings\i0 ), \b H\b0  (\i WordStar compatibility\i0 ), \b K\b0  (\i Vertical cursor movement\i0 , which is label SAMCOL).  Select \b 2\b0 .  Answer "n" to the "Are you through making changes?" question.  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li720\ri0\fi-720\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li720\ri0\fi-720\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 4.\tab Recommended:  To allow you to issue classic commands from WordStar's Help-Level-4 Opening Menu (such as "D" to open a document or "X to exit WordStar) without having to first activate a pulldown menu, go to WSCHANGE menu \b D\b0  (\i WordStar\i0 ), \b B\b0  (\i Editing settings\i0 ), \b H\b0  (\i WordStar compatibility\i0 ), \b I\b0  (\i Classic commands at Opening pull-down menu\i0 , which is label PULFLG).  Make sure this is set to \b ON\b0 .  (Note:  As of 6.0D, this feature is on by default, but if you've used WordStar's auto-patcher to transfer your customizations from a previous release of WordStar, this may have been reset to off.)}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 \tab Once again, issue ^X to jump to the WSCHANGE exit point, but this time answer "y" to the "Are you through making changes?" question.  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 {\*\bkmkstart _tc4}{\*\bkmkend _tc4}{\v {\field{\*\fldinst TC "Installation 2:  Modifying MICKEE" \\l 1}{\fldrslt }}}\b INSTALLATION 2:  MODIFYING MICKEE\b0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 \tab I've provided the annotated source code for MICKEE (file WS.DEF) for those who care to modify the way MICKEE works.  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 \tab To modify MICKEE, use WordStar to edit WS.DEF in \ul non- document\ulnone  mode.  There are a few items in WS.DEF you might particularly want to modify.  They are described below.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 \ul \i Mouse Sensitivity\ulnone \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 Near the beginning of WS.DEF are the settings for the horizontal and vertical mouse sensitivity.  If you prefer a more responsive mouse, decrease these numbers.  If a less responsive mouse is to your liking, increase these numbers.  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 \ul \i Cursor Movement in Text\ulnone \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24      Since most writing is word- rather than character-oriented, while editing MICKEE causes the cursor to jump left and right by whole words instead of characters.  This should simplify the marking of text blocks.  When the cursor is within a pulldown menu, a dialog box, or Advanced Page Preview, the left and right mouse movements are reprogrammed to correspond to the left and right arrow keys.  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 \tab If you don't like MICKEE moving the cursor by words instead of characters, open WS.DEF in non-document mode.  Find the lines that say:}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 \tab LMword:        TYPE 1                    ; ^A}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 \tab RMword:        TYPE 6                    ; ^F}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 Change them to say:}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 \tab LMword:        TYPE 0,75                 ; <Left Arrow>}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 \tab RMword:        TYPE 0,77                 ; <Right Arrow>}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 \ul \i Default Find Options\ulnone \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 \tab MICKEE'S "Mark Sentence" feature uses WordStar's own Find capability.  In so doing, it clears the values in the Find dialog box.  MICKEE is a polite rodent, though.  It resets the default Find options after each operation.  As supplied, MICKEE resets the defaults to \ul UA\ulnone , meaning "ignore case" and "align text if making a replacement."  If you would like to change these defaults, follow the instructions given in the WS.DEF file to edit the text of the line that begins "marksentb" (it's very near the end of the file).  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 \ul \i Valid Disk Drives\ulnone \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 \tab Finally, you may find that MICKEE's log-disk pop-up menu shows drive letters that are not valid on your system, or lists them in an order you don't like.  This pop-up is easily modifiable, and instructions are given in the WS.DEF file, starting with the line "Log-Drive pop-up."  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 \ul \i Compiling a New MICKEE\ulnone \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 \tab To make your changes take effect, you have to recompile MICKEE (that is, turn it into a form usable by the Logitech mouse).  Here's how:}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 \tab Copy WS.DEF to your mouse subdirectory, which should contain the Logitech program NEWMENU.EXE, and issue the command:}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\qc\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 C>NEWMENU WS}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 \tab That's it!  Your revised MICKEE is ready to roll.  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 \page {\*\bkmkstart _tc5}{\*\bkmkend _tc5}{\v {\field{\*\fldinst TC "Loading MICKEE" \\l 1}{\fldrslt }}}\b LOADING MICKEE\b0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 \tab If you use Logitech's CLICK.EXE to automatically switch mouse menus when you change programs, use WordStar's \ul non-document\ulnone  mode to edit the file CLICK.SRC, which came with your Logitech mouse.  If no entry for WS exists, add this line:  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\qc\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 WS menu 3buttons        ; WordStar 6.0D (MICKEE)}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 \tab If a WS entry does exist, then edit it to look like the one above.  You must then recompile CLICK using the Logitech NEWCLICK.EXE program.  Do that by issuing this command:}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\qc\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 C>NEWCLICK}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 \tab If you (1)\~store WS.MNU in your mouse subdirectory, and (2)\~have that subdirectory on your DOS PATH, and (3) run the Logitech programs MOUSE, LOGIMENU, and CLICK in your AUTOEXEC.BAT file, then MICKEE will load automatically each time you start WordStar.  (LOGIMENU.COM is the Logitech-supplied mouse-interface loader; note that if you have an older beige Logitech mouse, this program is simply called MENU.COM.)  However, I prefer not to use CLICK, because it takes up memory.  Instead, I like to start WordStar with a batch file containing these lines:}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\tx2160{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx2592{\f0\fs24 \tab c:\\mouse\\logimenu c:\\mouse\\ws}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx2592{\f0\fs24 \tab c:\\ws\\ws %1 %2 %3}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx2592{\f0\fs24 \tab c:\\mouse\\logimenu off}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 \tab (Substitute "menu" for "logimenu" if you have an older beige Logitech mouse; substitute the actual drive and subdirectory containing your WordStar program files for "c:\\ws"; substitute the actual drive and subdirectory containing your mouse files for "c:\\mouse".)  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 \tab The first line of this batch file loads MICKEE; the second loads WordStar with any filename and command-line switches you specify; the third shuts the mouse off when you exit WordStar so that MICKEE commands won't accidentally be activated in another program.  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 \tab I wrote MICKEE to be very frugal with your precious RAM, since WordStar demands so much of it.  MICKEE is content with just three kilobytes of RAM.  By default, Logitech's LOGIMENU.COM program sets aside a five-kilobyte buffer for the currently active compiled menu; you can reduce that to the three kilobytes MICKEE needs by loading LOGIMENU \ul the first time after you start your computer\ulnone  with this command line (perhaps in your AUTOEXEC.BAT file), freeing up two kilobytes of RAM:}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\qc\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 C>LOGIMENU /3}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 (Or, C>MENU /3, for older beige Logitech mice.)}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 \page {\*\bkmkstart _tc6}{\*\bkmkend _tc6}{\v {\field{\*\fldinst TC "Checking Which Release of MICKEE is Loaded" \\l 1}{\fldrslt }}}\b CHECKING WHICH RELEASE OF MICKEE IS LOADED\b0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 \tab To check which release of MICKEE you have loaded, open a file, issue ^KL (or use MICKEE to choose "Change drive / directory..." from the "File" pulldown menu), and press the middle mouse button.  You'll see your MICKEE release number to the left of the list of drive letters.  This manual is for release 6.D.4L, which only works with Logitech three-button pointing devices and WordStar 6.0 Rev. D or later.  To clear the release display, click on the happy face at the right end of the menu.  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 {\*\bkmkstart _tc7}{\*\bkmkend _tc7}{\v {\field{\*\fldinst TC "How MICKEE Knows What's Going On" \\l 1}{\fldrslt }}}\b HOW MICKEE KNOWS WHAT'S GOING ON\b0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 \tab MICKEE reads your screen while you're working.  If it sees the letter "W" (part of the word "WORDSTAR") on line 1 beginning at column 37, it knows you're at the Opening Menu.  If it sees an "E" (part of word "EDIT" at the right end of the EDIT pulldown menu bar) on line 2 beginning at column 73, it knows you're editing a file.  If you are editing a file, it then looks for a right-pointing arrowhead character at line 2, column 1.  If such a character is visible, MICKEE knows you've already activated a pulldown menu and programs the mouse buttons accordingly.  If that character is not visible, then MICKEE assumes your cursor is inside the document, and, again, programs the mouse buttons accordingly.  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 \tab MICKEE checks to see if you are inside a dialog box or Classic menu by looking for a \u9556? character (part of the border around Classic menus and dialog boxes) in line 2 at column 1.  If it finds one, it then checks to see which menu or dialog box you are in by reading the name off the screen, and then programs the buttons accordingly.  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 \tab If it doesn't find the \u9556? character, and has also been unable to find the "W" in WORDSTAR or the "E" in "EDIT," it then assumes you must be in Advanced Page Preview and once more reprograms the mouse buttons.  (Unfortunately, there is no way to suppress cursor wrapping in Advanced Page Preview's pulldown menus.)  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 \page {\*\bkmkstart _tc8}{\*\bkmkend _tc8}{\v {\field{\*\fldinst TC "Compatibility Notes" \\l 1}{\fldrslt }}}\b COMPATIBILITY NOTES\b0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 \tab MICKEE supports every standard WordStar screen installation, including the default 25-line mode; 43-line EGA mode; and 50-line VGA mode.  Indeed, MICKEE support any number of screen rows, so long as you are using 80 columns.  (The Logitech mouse software only supports 80-column screens.)}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \ul \i Non-English-Language WordStars\ulnone \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\keep{\f0\fs24 \tab MICKEE may fail if you are using a non-English-language version of WordStar, because the word "EDIT" in the pulldown menu bar may be different.  The following code fragment in WS.DEF appears several times.  It is responsible for finding the "E" at the beginning of the word "EDIT."  You can do a WordStar global search-and-replace of this code fragment to make MICKEE check for any other ASCII character instead.  Make sure the character you specify is not the same one that appears at screen row 2 column 73 in the OPENING pulldown menu bar.  If it is, try matching the character at a different screen column instead.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\qc\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 MATCH 2,73,,"E",}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 \tab MICKEE's pop-up menus are all in English, but can be changed by editing WS.DEF in non-document mode.  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \ul \i Non-Standard Menu Box-Drawing Characters\ulnone \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\keep{\f0\fs24 \tab MICKEE may also fail if you've changed the characters WordStar uses for drawing boxes around menus (WSCHANGE label BOXCHR), because it needs to be able to identify the \u9556? character that appears at the upper-left corner of menus and dialog boxes.  Because this character appears in WS.DEF as a naked extended character (without the ^[ and ^\\ fences WordStar normally puts around extended characters) you cannot change it to another extended character by using WordStar.  If you must change this character in WS.DEF, do so with a straight hexadecimal byte editor, such as NU.EXE, part of The Norton Utilities.  Alternatively, reset the default box-drawing characters by running WSCHANGE, selecting menu \b E\b0  (\i patching\i0 ), \b =\b0  (\i Enter User Area address\i0 ), specifying \b BOXCHR\b0 , and typing these six bytes:}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\qc\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 CD BA C9 C8 BB BC}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \ul \i UltraVision\ulnone \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\keep{\f0\fs24 \tab \ul UltraVision\ulnone , from Personics, improves EGA and VGA text quality in all applications, and gives users full control over their color palettes.  It also provides many non-standard screen sizes, and a wide choice of screen fonts.  You can use MICKEE under UltraVision with whichever font you like, and with any number of rows, but because of the limitations of the Logitech software noted above, MICKEE will only work on 80-column screens.  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \ul \i ScreenExtender\ulnone \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\keep{\f0\fs24 \tab \ul ScreenExtender\ulnone , from Stairway Software, lets you dynamically reset screen dimensions from within WordStar.  ScreenExtender causes WordStar to work in graphics mode.  This makes it incompatible with MICKEE, which must be able to read the screen in text mode to know in which part of WordStar you are working.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 {\*\bkmkstart _tc9}{\*\bkmkend _tc9}{\v {\field{\*\fldinst TC "Mouse Interfaces and InSet" \\l 1}{\fldrslt }}}\b MOUSE INTERFACES AND INSET\b0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 \tab InSet, the graphics-integration program included with WordStar\~6.0, is mouse-aware.  However, it's also a bully.  If you activate InSet while editing (either by issuing ^P& or by selecting "Start InSet" from the "Other" Edit Pulldown menu), your mouse will work fine while you are in InSet (the left button will issue the equivalent of <Enter>; the middle button, <Space>; the right button, <Esc>).  However, when you leave InSet and return to editing, InSet will shut off MICKEE (or any other compiled Logitech mouse interface).  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 \tab Charles Edwards has come up with a fix for this.  His fix is extensively documented in the file LOGINS.ARC in Library 7 (6/5.5 Preview/Grphcs) of the CompuServe WordStar Forum.  If you have access to CompuServe, I strongly recommend you download Charles's file and read his entire instructions.  In case you don't have a CompuServe account, here's a quick version of what Charles came up with.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 \tab Charles's fix has only been tested by him with versions 3.41}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 and 4.00 of the Logitech mouse software, and by me with version 4.10.  Although the instructions he provides are for WordStar 5.5C, they seem to work equally well with 6.0D.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 \tab \ul Warnings\ulnone :  (1) If you make this patch, you should \ul always\ulnone  run WordStar with Logitech's MOUSE.COM loaded.  If you don't and particularly if you use an early version of MS-DOS (such as 2.0), your system may crash when you invoke InSet.  (2) MICKEE will be temporarily disabled when printing a document in the background that contains InSet graphics.  (3) MICKEE will be disabled for the remainder of your editing session if you print-to-disk a document containing InSet graphics.  (4) You can't use this fix if you're already using WordStar's EXTRA patch area for something else.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 \tab Make a back-up copy of your current version of WS.EXE and store it in a safe place.  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\keep{\f0\fs24 \tab Use WordStar's \ul non-document\ulnone  mode to create a file named LOGINSET.PAT in the directory that contains your WordStar program files.  Type these five lines exactly, and make sure the last line ends with a hard carriage return (alternatively, open the file containing this manual, MICKEE.DOC, and block write these lines to a file):}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 INISUB=E9,AB,0D}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 UNISUB=E9,B3,0D}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 ULUNPT=E9,14,0A}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 EXTRA=B8,00,00,CD,33,B8,6C,29,CD,33}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\keep{\f0\fs24 =C3,3D,03,00,74,05,B8,00,00,CD,33,C3}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 Go to WSCHANGE menu \b E\b0  (\i Patching\i0 ), \b A\b0  (\i Patch from file\i0 ), and specify the filename LOGINSET.PAT.  When patching is complete, exit WSCHANGE. }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \tab As I said, Charles has provided much more detailed and complete instructions in his file LOGINS.ARC and I recommend that you acquire this file and read it.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720\keep{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 \tab If you don't want to make the patch described above, you can still work around InSet's bad manners.  Assuming your mouse subdirectory is included in your DOS PATH statement, you can reload MICKEE after using InSet by temporarily exiting to DOS with ^KF and running:}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\qc\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 C>LOGIMENU WS}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 (or C>MENU WS, if yours is an older beige mouse).  This command sequence can be placed in a Shorthand macro, if you like.  (If you do so, you may wish to turn off WordStar's prompt to "Press any key to return to WordStar."  This is a new option with WordStar 6.0D, available at WSCHANGE menu \b D\b0  (\i WordStar\i0 ), \b B\b0  (\i Editing settings\i0 ), \b A\b0  (\i Edit screen, help level\i0 ), \b 3\b0  (\i Edit screen, help level menu\~#3\i0 ), \b J\b0  (\i Turn "Return to WordStar" prompt on or off\i0 , which is label RUNMSG).  Set it to \b OFF\b0 .  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 \tab Or, if you prefer, after running InSet, simply save and exit WordStar with ^KX then run WordStar again.  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 \page {\*\bkmkstart _tc10}{\*\bkmkend _tc10}{\v {\field{\*\fldinst TC "Opening Menu Tips and Tricks" \\l 1}{\fldrslt }}}\b OPENING MENU TIPS AND TRICKS\b0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 \tab Many old-time WordStar users dislike the new Help-Level-4 Opening Menu because the <Esc> key does not work to activate Shorthand macros, even if "Classic commands at Opening pull-down menu" is turned on in WSCHANGE (which, starting with 6.0D, is the default).  Rich Zuris of WordStar International has provided a workaround for this:  with Classic commands on, \b [\b0  (the opening square bracket character) will activate Shorthand from the Opening Menu.  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 \tab There is one big undocumented advantage to using Help-Level 4 at the Opening Menu, as discovered by Ed Zollars of the CompuServe WordStar Forum:  you do not first have to pull down a menu to specify that you want to open a file in the default editing mode or that you want to change the logged subdirectory.  Simply point and shoot.  If you point at a filename, WordStar will open that file; if you point at a subdirectory name, WordStar will log onto that subdirectory.  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 \tab I found another undocumented goody myself.  If you want to change help levels, and have "Classic commands at Opening pull- down menu" turned on, rather than using the "Change help level" command on the Pulldown "Other" menu, you can simply type \b H\b0  followed by the help level number you want.  \b H2\b0  will drop you down to Help-Level 2; \b H4\b0  will scoot you back up to Help-Level 4.  (One reason to temporarily drop down is that there is no Help- Level-4 equivalent to the Classic command FF, which sets the directory filename mask.)}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 \page {\*\bkmkstart _tc11}{\*\bkmkend _tc11}{\v {\field{\*\fldinst TC "Using MICKEE at the Opening Menu" \\l 1}{\fldrslt }}}\b Using MICKEE at the Opening Menu\b0 }\par
