\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 \column The \ul Left\ulnone  button \b selects\b0  the word under the cursor in the thesaurus window.  If that word is a synonym for the word in the document, the synonym is swapped into the underlying document, replacing the original word, and the thesaurus window is closed.  If instead you are looking at the screen that says "Word not found\~-- near alphabet guesses," then the left button will look up synonyms for the highlighted near-alphabetic word.  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 The \ul Middle\ulnone  button gives you \b definitions\b0  for the word under the cursor, assuming definitions are available for that word.  The first press gives you the first definition.  If that's the only definition, the definition will end with a period.  If there is more than one definition available, the definition will end with a semicolon.  In that case, subsequent presses of the middle button will bring up the remaining definitions.  When you reach the last definition, you can return to the main thesaurus window by pressing the right button.  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 The \ul Right\ulnone  button \b exits\b0  the thesaurus (or leaves the definition function and returns to thesaurus, if you were looking at definitions).  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 Pressing the \ul Left and Middle\ulnone  buttons simultaneously \b cross-references\b0  the highlighted word, just as if you'd typed \b L\b0 .  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 Pressing the \ul Middle and Right\ulnone  buttons simultaneously returns to the \b previous word\b0 , just as if you'd typed \b P\b0 .  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\fi-144\sl-240\slmult0\tx720{\f0\fs24 }\par
